# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vt [command [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vt` runs a program that wants a terminal, one that writes escape sequences and reads keys as xterm sends them, inside the rio(1) window it is run in, as Plan 9's vt does.
It runs `rc -i` if no command is given; a command without a `/` is found in `/bin`.
A rio window shows plain text; `vt` is for the programs that want more.

`vt` takes the window over without a window of its own: it opens a view the window's size, from the window's `wctl`, and lends it to the window through the window's `surface` (rio(4), rio-surface(5)), which shows it over its text until it closes.
It turns raw mode on with `/dev/consctl`'s `rawon`, so the window's keys come to it as they are typed, through `rc`, on its standard input: characters as they are, and, while the surface is lent, the arrows, Home, End, Page Up, Page Down and the function keys as kbdfs(4)'s runes.
It sends each to the program as xterm does: Enter as a carriage return, Backspace as DEL, Delete as `ESC [ 3 ~`, the arrows as `ESC [ A` or, in application cursor mode, `ESC O A`, and so on.
Delete doesn't interrupt while `vt` runs; the program gets it.

The program runs on standard input and output rings (its standard error is its output), in a namespace of `vt`'s with an environment group of its own, holding `TERM=xterm-256color` and the screen's size as `COLUMNS` and `LINES`, and in a job of its own.
There is no pty: its output is read as it comes and drawn on a screen of the view's size in cells of the theme's mono face, in the theme's `terminal` part.
The screen is the part of xterm that programs use, through the DEC parser's states (TerminalCore): the C0 controls BEL, BS, HT, LF, VT, FF and CR; ESC's 7, 8, D, E, H, M and c; CSI's cursor movements, erasing, inserting and deleting characters and lines, scrolling, the scroll region, tab stops and the modes IRM and LNM; DEC's modes 1 (cursor keys), 6 (origin), 7 (wrap), 25 (cursor), 47, 1047 and 1049 (the alternate screen) and 2004 (paste); SGR's attributes and colours, 16 from the theme's roles `term.0` to `term.15`, xterm's 256 and 24-bit; DSR 5 and 6 and DA, answered; and OSC 0 and 2 (the title) and 7 (the program's directory).
Anything else is parsed and dropped, DCS strings among it.
Of the attributes, underline, inverse and hidden change what is drawn; bold, dim, italic, blink and strike are kept but drawn plain.
Lines scrolled off the top are kept as scrollback, which the wheel and the bar scroll through; output brings the view back down.
A right-click's Route sends the word under the pointer to the plumber, from the directory the program last gave in OSC 7.
When the window is resized, the screen takes the view's new size; the program learns it only by asking, as with a cursor position report after moving the cursor past the corner.

When the program ends, `vt` writes the main screen's lines to its standard output, so the window keeps them as text; the view closes, the window shows its text again, and raw mode ends.

`vt-test` is the boot test boot-vt's program, and the program it runs, as `vt-test child`.
The driver types `vt vt-test child` into rio's first window through QEMU's keys, sees no new window in Radharc's list and the window's text untouched while `vt` has it, resizes the window, and types `r`, Up and `q`.
The child writes colours, addresses the cursor and moves back over what it wrote, reports `TERM` and its size, and in the alternate screen with application cursor keys measures its screen at the start and at `r`, and reads keys until `q`.
After `vt` ends, the driver finds the child's lines in the window's text, Up as `ESC O A`, the screen the size the environment said and smaller after the resize, nothing of the alternate screen, and `rc` with the window back.

